    "result_text": "I implemented `clean_orders` in `app/clean.py`. The existing test passes, and a scratch check confirmed the behaviour, including that the input frame is left unchanged.\n\n**What it does:**\n- It works on a copy of the frame.\n- **Text columns:** it trims leading and trailing spaces, and empty strings (including ones that were only spaces) become missing values. This covers pandas 3's default `str` columns, the `\"string\"` dtype, and general object columns. In object columns, only actual strings are changed; any numbers or `None` in them stay as they are.\n- **`qty`:** missing values become 0. The column keeps its type: a float column stays float, and an `Int64` column stays `Int64`.\n- **Everything else** (such as `price`) is left alone. Missing prices stay missing.\n\n**What I checked:** I ran it on a CSV loaded through `load_orders`, on an object column holding mixed values, and on a nullable `string` / `Int64` frame. All three gave the expected output, and the dtypes were unchanged.\n\nThe only existing test just checks that `clean_orders` can be called, so the actual cleaning behaviour isn't covered by the test suite. I didn't add tests.\n\n**One thing to clean up:** my scratch script `_scratch.py` is still in the project root. The delete command needed approval, so it didn't run. It's safe to delete, or I can remove it if you approve.\n\nI checked the pandas 3.0 docs through Context7 for how to select string columns and how the new string dtype behaves. No dependencies were added."
